.docname {Notification Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Breach | A reading past its limit, sent by `telemetry` | 31 °C, limit 28 °C |
    | Alert record | One breach as a Kafka message on `alerts` | An `AlertEvent` keyed `b1:r1` |
    | Cooldown | A Redis key that mutes repeats of one breach for 300 s | `alert:temperature:temperature:b1:r1` |
    | Domain | The organisation area a building belongs to; alerts go to its members | `eng` |
    | Bell | The in-app message list in the browser, fed through `socket` | "Temperature Alert - HQ" |
    | Web Push | A pop-up the browser shows even with the tab closed | Sent through `fcm.googleapis.com` |
    | Push subscription | One device's push address and keys | `{endpoint, keys: {p256dh, auth}}` |
    | Preference | One account's on/off switch for one metric in one domain | Ada, `eng`, `airQuality`, on |
    | Audience | Which domains a caller may reach | `Domains(["eng"])` or `Unrestricted` |
    | Dead-letter topic | Kafka topic for records the service cannot use | `alerts.dlq` |
    | Watermark | The highest offset below which every record is done | 41 when 42 is still running |

Rust service at `backend/notification`. Axum, MongoDB, Kafka, Redis. It reads breaches from Kafka, decides who should hear about them, and delivers each one twice: to the bell over Redis, and to devices over Web Push. It also stores devices and preferences, and sends manual alerts.

Role in the system: [Notification](../architecture/notification-architecture.qd). Breach shape and how it is parsed: [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd). Message shape: [Notification Schema](../packages/notification-schema.qd).

---

## Layout

Ports and adapters. `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` fails the build when a layer imports the wrong way.

| Path | Holds | May not import |
|---|---|---|
| `src/domain/` | `notification()`, `breach_message`, `breach_cooldown_key`, `breach_push_title`; `GatewayClaims`, `Audience`; preferences; push subscriptions; `DomainError` | any framework, `crate::service`, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/service/` | Use cases `Alerts`, `Push`, `Preferences`; `ports.rs`; `fakes.rs` for tests | any framework, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/adapters/driving/` | `http_api/` (handlers, claims extractor, error mapping), `alert_listener.rs` (Kafka) | `crate::adapters::driven` |
| `src/adapters/driven/` | Mongo stores, Web Push sender, Redis bus and cooldown, digital-twin lookup | |
| `src/adapters/ratelimit.rs` | 300 requests per minute per client IP | |
| `src/main.rs` | Reads config, connects, wires everything, starts the listener and the server | |

The same test checks that the claims header name only comes from `claims_schema::CLAIMS_HEADER`, which must equal `x-gateway-claims`.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        subgraph Driving
            H["http_api"]
            L["alert_listener"]
        end
        subgraph Service
            A["Alerts"]
            P["Push"]
            Pr["Preferences"]
        end
        subgraph Driven
            M["MongoSubscriptions<br/>MongoPreferences"]
            R["RedisBus<br/>publish + cooldown"]
            W["WebPushSender"]
            T["TwinDirectory"]
        end
        H --> A
        H --> Pr
        L --> A
        A --> P
        A -. "NotificationBus, Cooldown" .-> R
        A -. "DomainDirectory" .-> T
        P -. "SubscriptionStore, PreferenceStore" .-> M
        P -. "PushSender" .-> W
        Pr -. "stores" .-> M

Dotted: through a port trait in `service/ports.rs`.

---

## Routes

All behind the edge at `/notification/*`. Every route except the public ones needs the `x-gateway-claims` header with a non-blank `accountName`, else `401`.

| Method | Path | Who may call | What it does | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/health`, `/metrics` | Anyone | Probe, Prometheus | `200` |
| `GET` | `/public-key` | Anyone | The VAPID public key a browser needs to create a push subscription | `{publicVapidKey}` |
| `POST` | `/subscribe` | Signed-in account | Store this device; with a domain, also switch preferences on | `201 {success: true}` |
| `GET` | `/preferences`, `/preferences/{accountName}` | Signed-in account | The caller's own preferences. The path name is ignored | `{success, accountPreferences}` |
| `POST` | `/preferences` | Member of the named domain | Set one or more switches | `{success: true}` |
| `POST` | `/trigger` | Signed-in account | Manual alert for a building, no cooldown | `{success, message: "Notification sent"}` |

The account always comes from the claims, never from the body or the path. A body with `"accountName": "mallory"` from Ada still writes to Ada.

### Errors

`DomainError` becomes `{type, message}`:

| Variant | Status | `type` |
|---|---|---|
| `Validation` | 400 | `Validation Error` |
| `Unauthorized` | 401 | `Unauthorized Error` |
| `Forbidden` | 403 | `Forbidden Error` |
| `NotFound` | 404 | `Not Found Error` |
| `Internal` | 500 | `Internal Server Error`; the message is generic, the cause only logged |

A client over 300 requests in 60 s gets `429`. The client IP is the **rightmost** `x-forwarded-for` entry, the one the edge appended, so a forged left entry cannot reset the count.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `MONGO_URI` | `mongodb://localhost:27017/notificationdb` | Database; its name comes from the URI path, else `notificationdb` |
| `REDIS_URL` | `redis://localhost:6379` | Bell channel `notifications` and cooldown keys |
| `KAFKA_BROKERS` | `kafka:9092` | Source of `alerts`; target of `alerts.dlq` |
| `DIGITAL_TWIN_URL` | `http://localhost:3000` | Owner lookup `GET /domain/{building}` |
| `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` | empty | Push signing keys. Empty: every push fails and is logged, nothing panics |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port |

Startup order in `main.rs`:

1. Connect to MongoDB and create the indexes. Failure stops the service.
2. Connect to Redis. Failure stops the service.
3. Run `switch_on_new_metrics_once`. Failure is logged only.
4. Spawn the Kafka listener. A broker down at start is not fatal: the listener retries every 5 s.
5. Serve HTTP with graceful shutdown on Ctrl+C or SIGTERM.

---

## What it stores

MongoDB, two collections plus one bookkeeping collection:

| Collection | One document per | Fields | Indexes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `webpushsubscriptions` | Device | `accountName`, `endpoint`, `keys {p256dh, auth}` | unique `endpoint`; unique `accountName + endpoint`; `accountName` |
| `notificationsubscriptions` | Account and domain | `accountName`, `domainName`, `preferences [{notificationType, isSubscribed}]`, `createdAt` | unique `accountName + domainName`; `domainName` |
| `migrations` | One-time change | `_id` like `switched-on:airQuality`, `at` | |

Redis holds only cooldown keys, value `"1"`, expiry 300 s. Nothing else is kept: alerts are not stored.

---

## 1. A breach

### End to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant K as Kafka alerts
        participant L as alert_listener
        participant A as Alerts::on_breach
        participant R as Redis
        participant T as TwinDirectory
        participant P as Push::to_domain
        participant DB as MongoDB
        participant W as WebPushSender
        K->>L: record, key b1:r1
        L->>L: Watermarks::started(partition, offset)
        L->>A: handle: bytes to text, then on_breach(text)
        A->>A: from_str into AlertEvent
        A->>A: is_alertable()
        A->>R: GET alert:temperature:temperature:b1:r1
        R-->>A: none
        A->>A: breach_message(alert)
        A->>T: domains_for_building(b1, system claims)
        T-->>A: eng, from cache or GET /domain/b1
        loop each domain
            A->>R: PUBLISH notifications, Notification with metric
            A->>P: to_domain(notification, eng, temperature)
            P->>DB: accounts with temperature on in eng
            P->>DB: their push subscriptions
            P->>W: send to every device at once
        end
        A->>R: SET cooldown key to 1, EX 300
        A-->>L: Delivered
        L->>L: Watermarks::finished, commit offset + 1

### Data at each step

Example: room `r1` of building `b1` ("HQ", room "Lab 2") reaches 31 °C, limit 28 °C. Ada is a member of `eng` with temperature on and two devices.

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `{"buildingId":"b1","roomId":"r1","buildingName":"HQ","roomName":"Lab 2","temperature":31.0,"type":"temperature","field":"temperature","label":"Temperature","unit":"°C","direction":"high","threshold":28.0,"timestamp":1700000000000}` |
| 4 | `AlertEvent` with `metric` `temperature`, `field` `temperature`, `value` 31.0, `direction` `Above` |
| 6 | Key `alert:temperature:temperature:b1:r1` |
| 8 | Message `HQ : Lab 2 Temperature is 31 °C (above maximum)`. Push title `Temperature Alert - HQ` |
| 9 | Header = base64 of `{"sub":"system:notification-service","memberships":[]}` |
| 11 | `{"id":"1700000000123","type":"danger","title":"Temperature Alert - HQ","message":"HQ : Lab 2 Temperature is 31 °C (above maximum)","timestamp":"2023-11-14T22:13:20.123Z","domainName":"eng","metric":"temperature"}` |
| 13 | `["ada"]` |
| 14 | Ada's 2 subscriptions |
| 15 | 2 encrypted POSTs, same JSON as step 11 |
| 16 | `SET alert:temperature:temperature:b1:r1 "1" EX 300` |

A missing unit drops the space and unit: `HQ : Lab 2 Air Quality is 140 (above maximum)`. A low breach ends in `(below minimum)`.

### Every outcome

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        S["record"] --> U{"UTF-8 text?"}
        U -- no --> Pk1["park: undecodable"]
        U -- yes --> Pa{"parses as AlertEvent?"}
        Pa -- no --> Pk2["Invalid: park"]
        Pa -- yes --> Al{"metric in ALERTABLE_METRICS?"}
        Al -- no --> Un["Unsupported: settle"]
        Al -- yes --> Cd{"cooldown key read"}
        Cd -- "read failed" --> Pk3["Failed: park"]
        Cd -- "set" --> Su["Suppressed: settle"]
        Cd -- "not set" --> Lk{"owner lookup"}
        Lk -- failed --> Pk3
        Lk -- "no domains" --> Ur["Unroutable: drop, arm cooldown, settle"]
        Lk -- "1 or more" --> De["Delivered: publish and push, arm cooldown, settle"]

| Outcome | Metric label | Record | Cooldown armed |
|---|---|---|---|
| Not text | `undecodable` | parked | no |
| `Invalid` | `invalid` | parked | no |
| `Unsupported` | `unsupported_metric` | settled | no |
| `Failed` | `failed` | parked | no |
| `Suppressed` | `suppressed` | settled | already |
| `Unroutable` | `unroutable` | settled | yes |
| `Delivered` | `delivered` | settled | yes |

- **Parked** = copied to `alerts.dlq` with a `reason` header, then committed. If the park itself fails, the offset is not committed, so Kafka delivers the record again.
- **Never broadcast.** A building in no domain is dropped: a message with no domain would reach every tenant.
- **Push errors never change the outcome.** A failed Redis publish or a dead device is logged, and the outcome is still `Delivered`.
- `Failed` arms no cooldown, so the next breach of the same room tries again at once.

### The cooldown

- Key: `alert:{metric}:{field}:{buildingId}:{roomId}`. An empty id becomes `unknown`.
- One key per field, so a CO₂ alert does not mute a temperature or AQI alert in the same room.
- The key is read first and set **after** delivery. Two records of the same breach handled at the same moment can both pass the read, so both deliver.

**Example.** `telemetry` reports 31 °C every 10 s from 10:00:00.

| Time | Record | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| 10:00:00 | 1st | Delivered, key set until 10:05:00 |
| 10:00:10 to 10:04:50 | 29 more | Suppressed |
| 10:05:00 | next | Delivered again, key set until 10:10:00 |

A breach that lasts an hour sends 12 alerts.

### Finding the owners

`TwinDirectory::domains_for_building`, in `adapters/driven/twin.rs`:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant A as caller
        participant T as TwinDirectory
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        A->>T: domains_for_building(b1, header)
        T->>T: cached(b1), younger than 15 min?
        alt cached
            T-->>A: domains
        else not cached
            T->>T: lock gate(b1), one lookup per building
            T->>T: cached(b1) again
            T->>Tw: GET /domain/b1, timeout 2 s
            alt network error or 5xx
                T->>T: wait 50 to 100 ms
                T->>Tw: GET /domain/b1, one retry
            end
            Tw-->>T: [eng]
            T->>T: remember(b1) only when not empty
            T-->>A: [eng]
        end

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| 2 s timeout | A hung digital-twin must fail the lookup, not stall the consumer |
| One retry on network error or 5xx only | A dropped connection should not cost an alert; a 404 will not change |
| Gate per building | 16 records for the same building at once make 1 request, not 16 |
| Cache 15 min, only non-empty answers | Ownership rarely changes; a building just being registered is asked again next time |
| Answer cleaned by `unique_non_empty` | Duplicates and empty names never become rooms |

**Example.** 5 breaches from `b1` arrive together with an empty cache. The first takes the gate and calls digital-twin; it answers after 300 ms. The other 4 wait on the gate, find the cache filled, and return without a call. digital-twin sees 1 request.

### Committing the offset

The listener handles up to 16 records at once (`for_each_concurrent`) and commits by hand (`enable.auto.commit=false`).

`Watermarks` keeps, per partition, the offsets running and the offsets done. Only the run of done offsets below the lowest running one is committed.

**Example.** Offsets 40, 41, 42 start. 42 finishes first: nothing is committed, since 40 and 41 run. 40 finishes: commit 41 (next to read). 41 finishes: commit 43.

If the service dies between, Kafka delivers again from the last commit. The cooldown stops most of the repeats from reaching people.

Consumer group: `alerts`, `auto.offset.reset=earliest`. Before subscribing, `ensure_topics` creates `alerts` and `alerts.dlq` with 1 partition when missing: subscribing to a topic that does not exist waits for a 5 min metadata refresh.

---

## 2. Push delivery

`Push` in `service/push.rs`, called once per domain:

1. `to_domain(payload, domain, type)` asks `PreferenceStore::accounts_subscribed_to(domain, type)`:
   - with a type: documents of the domain with an element `{isSubscribed: true, notificationType: type}`;
   - without a type: any element with `isSubscribed: true`.
2. Account names are made unique.
3. `to_accounts` loads every subscription of those accounts (`accountName $in`).
4. Every device is sent at the same time (`join_all`).
5. Each send ends in one of three outcomes:

| Push service answers | Outcome | Action |
|---|---|---|
| 2xx | `Delivered` | none |
| 404 or 410 | `SubscriptionGone` | delete the subscription by endpoint |
| any other status, network error, 10 s timeout, encryption error | `Failed` | log only, keep it |

A 403 means the push service refused the VAPID signature: a wrong or rotated key. Deleting on it would wipe every device at once, so it is kept.

The payload is the `Notification` JSON, encrypted with `aes128gcm`, signed with VAPID, with a `TTL` header.

### The bell and the switches

- The bell message goes to the **whole domain** room and carries `metric`.
- The browser hides a breach whose metric the account switched off (`isVisible` in `frontend/src/utils/commons/notification.ts`).
- Push applies the same rule on the server, through `notificationType` in step 1.
- A manual alert has no `metric`: the bell always shows it.

---

## 3. Registering a device

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as subscribe handler
        participant D as WebPushSubscription::new
        participant Pr as Preferences
        participant DB as MongoDB
        B->>H: POST /subscribe {endpoint, keys, domainName}
        H->>H: GatewayClaims from x-gateway-claims
        H->>H: take subscription object, else top-level endpoint and keys
        H->>D: new(account, endpoint, p256dh, auth)
        D-->>H: ok, or 400
        H->>Pr: register_device
        Pr->>DB: find_one_and_replace by endpoint, upsert
        alt domainName or domainId given
            H->>H: require_membership(claims, domain)
            H->>H: resolve_lenient(account, domain)
            H->>Pr: apply(updates)
            Pr->>DB: set, once per type
        end
        H-->>B: 201 {success: true}

Checks in `WebPushSubscription::new`:

| Check | Fails with |
|---|---|
| Account not blank | `Invalid authenticated account` |
| `endpoint`, `p256dh`, `auth` present | `Invalid push subscription payload` |
| `endpoint` is `https`, default port, host is or ends in `.fcm.googleapis.com`, `.push.services.mozilla.com`, `.push.apple.com`, `.notify.windows.com` | `Push endpoint must be an https URL on a known push service` |

The server POSTs to the endpoint, so an arbitrary URL would let a caller point the service at any address.

- **The device is stored before the membership check.** A domain the caller is not in gives `403`, but the device stays registered, with no preferences for that domain.
- The upsert key is the endpoint. One browser that signs in as another account moves its endpoint to that account.

**Example.** Ada, member of `eng`, sends `{"endpoint":"https://fcm.googleapis.com/fcm/send/abc","keys":{"p256dh":"BN…","auth":"k3…"},"domainName":"eng"}` with no type. `resolve_lenient` switches on `temperature`, `airQuality` and `peopleCount`. Answer `201`.

---

## 4. Changing preferences

`POST /preferences` takes one of three body forms, all pinned in `schemas/fixtures/notification-preferences.json`:

| Form | Example | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Single | `{"domainName":"eng","type":"airQuality","enabled":false}` | 1 update |
| List | `{"domainName":"eng","types":["temperature","peopleCount"],"enabled":true}` | 2 updates |
| Granular | `{"domainName":"eng","preferences":[{"type":"temperature","enabled":true},{"type":"airQuality","enabled":false}]}` | 2 updates |

`domainId` is accepted in place of `domainName`.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        B["body"] --> Dn{"domainName or domainId?"}
        Dn -- no --> E1["400 domainName is required"]
        Dn -- yes --> M{"Audience permits domain?"}
        M -- no --> E2["403"]
        M -- yes --> G{"preferences[] has an entry without type?"}
        G -- yes --> E3["400 type is required"]
        G -- no --> Gr{"preferences[] not empty?"}
        Gr -- yes --> U1["one update per entry"]
        Gr -- no --> Li{"types[] not empty?"}
        Li -- yes --> En1{"enabled given?"}
        En1 -- no --> E4["400 enabled is required"]
        En1 -- yes --> U2["one update per type"]
        Li -- no --> En2{"enabled given?"}
        En2 -- no --> E4
        En2 -- yes --> Ty{"type given?"}
        Ty -- no --> E3
        Ty -- yes --> U3["one update"]
        U1 & U2 & U3 --> V{"every type in ALERTABLE_METRICS?"}
        V -- no --> E6["400 type must be one of: temperature, airQuality, peopleCount"]
        V -- yes --> W["apply: set, one by one"]

`/subscribe` uses `resolve_lenient` instead: no type means every alertable metric, and no `enabled` means on.

### How one switch is written

`MongoPreferences::set`, two writes:

1. `$pull` any entry with this `notificationType` from the `{accountName, domainName}` document.
2. `$push` the new `{notificationType, isSubscribed}`; `$setOnInsert` creates the document with `createdAt` on first write (`upsert`).

So writing the same switch twice leaves one entry. The two writes are not atomic: a crash between them leaves the type missing, which reads as off.

`apply` writes updates one after the other and stops at the first error, so a list can be half applied.

### New metrics for old accounts

`switch_on_new_metrics_once` runs at every start:

1. For each alertable metric except `temperature`, look for marker `switched-on:{metric}` in `migrations`. Found: skip.
2. Else, for every document with temperature on and no entry for this metric, push `{notificationType: metric, isSubscribed: true}`.
3. Store the marker.

A stored choice always wins: a document that already has the metric is not touched. Accounts that subscribed before the metric existed get it on, once.

---

## 5. Manual alert

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as Caller
        participant H as trigger_alert
        participant A as Alerts::trigger
        participant T as TwinDirectory
        participant R as Redis
        participant P as Push
        C->>H: POST /trigger {buildingName, message, type, notificationType}
        H->>A: trigger(…, claims.raw, Audience::of(claims))
        A->>A: message defaults to Manual Alert Triggered
        A->>A: type must parse as Severity, default danger
        A->>A: buildingName required
        A->>T: domains_for_building(building, caller's header)
        T-->>A: domains
        A->>A: permitted_by(domains, audience)
        loop each permitted domain
            A->>R: PUBLISH notifications, title CrowdVision Alert
            A->>P: to_domain(notification, domain, notificationType)
        end
        A-->>H: Ok
        H-->>C: 200 {success, message: Notification sent}

- No cooldown: every call delivers.
- `type` is a severity, `info`, `warning` or `danger`. Anything else is `400 type must be one of: info, warning, danger`.
- `notificationType` is optional. Absent, push reaches every subscriber of the domain with any switch on.
- A lookup failure is `500`.

### Who may reach which domain

`Audience::of(claims)`:

| Caller | Audience |
|---|---|
| `sub` starts with `system:` | `Unrestricted` |
| Holds the role `admin` in any membership | `Unrestricted` |
| Anyone else | `Domains` = the domains in their memberships |

Membership does not reach down to sub-domains: a member of `unibo` is not, by that alone, a member of a sub-domain of `unibo`.

`trigger` filters digital-twin's answer itself, because `GET /domain/{building}` does not limit the answer to the caller's domains.

| Building's domains | Caller's domains | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `eng`, `finance` | `eng` | 200, delivered to `eng` only |
| `finance` | `eng` | 403 `Not a member of any domain for this building` |
| none | `eng` | 200, nothing delivered |
| `finance` | admin | 200, delivered to `finance` |

`digital-twin` calls `/trigger` as `system:digital-twin` when a registration fails, so its alert reaches every domain of the building.

The admin rule is a role check in this service, not a Cedar decision: the service does not embed the Cedar bundle. The test `the_admin_role_is_the_top_of_the_shared_ladder` reads `backend/libs/auth-contracts/roles.json` and fails if a role above `admin` appears.

The breach path takes no caller. It looks up owners with the system identity, whose memberships are empty, and does not filter by audience. The audience filter lives in `trigger` and the handlers only; moving it into the shared `fan_out` would silence every breach.

---

## Tests

```bash
just test notification               # unit, no infra
just test notification-integration   # tests/*.rs against throwaway Kafka, Mongo, Redis
```

| Where | Checks |
|---|---|
| `service/alerts.rs` | Every outcome; cooldown per field; no domain never published; a breach compared to `schemas/fixtures/notification.json` |
| `service/push.rs` | All devices of all named accounts; `Gone` deleted; other failures kept |
| `service/preferences.rs` | Same endpoint once; same type not duplicated; later write wins; reads only own records |
| `http_api/controllers.rs` | Every route without claims is `401`; body account ignored; membership `403`s; admin and system reach; every fixture request accepted and every rejection refused with its reason |
| `driven/twin.rs` | Encoding, 2 s timeout, cache, no cache for empty or failed, one retry on 5xx, none on 404, one request for 5 concurrent misses |
| `driven/push.rs` | 201 delivered, 404 and 410 gone, other statuses failed, timeout, VAPID header, empty keys never panic |
| `alert_listener.rs` | Watermark commits only a contiguous run |
| `tests/alerts_flow.rs` | Breach produced while the service is off still delivered on return; second breach in cooldown publishes nothing; a record that can never be handled lands on `alerts.dlq` |
| `tests/persistence.rs` | Indexes and queries on a real Mongo; new metrics switched on exactly once |
| `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` | Layer imports; claims header name |

---

## Gaps

- **`alerts.dlq` has no reader.** Parked records stay there until the topic's retention removes them.
- **The cooldown check is not atomic.** Read, deliver, then set: two copies of one breach handled together both deliver.
- **A long breach alerts every 5 minutes.** `telemetry` sends one record per breaching reading, and nothing marks the end of a breach.
- **Preference writes are two steps.** A crash between `$pull` and `$push` loses that switch, which then reads as off.
- **A forbidden domain on `/subscribe` still stores the device.** The answer is `403`, which can read as "nothing stored".
